  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed breakdown of why nitrogen is used in plastic welding:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd35 1. **Prevents Oxidation (The Main Reason)**\nNitrogen is used for plastic welding because it creates a stronger, cleaner weld compared to hot air. Air is composed of 80% nitrogen and 20% oxygen, and that oxygen causes oxidation, burning the plastic during the welding process. This results in smoke, ash, and sparks, which weaken the weld and create contamination.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd35 2. **Acts as a Shielding Gas**\nA nitrogen hot air welder uses compressed nitrogen gas to eliminate oxygen from the weld area. The nitrogen acts as a shielding gas and allows for a contaminant-free weld with less smoke, which creates a stronger weld. This is similar in concept to the shielding gases used in MIG welding of metals.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd35 3. **Produces a Cleaner, Stronger Weld**\nWelding with nitrogen eliminates oxidation issues by flushing out the oxygen from the weld zone. Without oxygen, the plastic melts cleanly without burning or oxidizing, allowing the melted plastics to fuse together with no contamination for a stronger, more durable weld.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd35 4. **Reduces Burning and Smoke**\nHot air welding produces significant smoke, while nitrogen generates minimal smoke. Regular hot air welding causes plastic to glow and burn, while nitrogen acts as a shielding gas, preventing oxidation and reducing the risk of burning.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd35 5. **Better Thermodynamic Properties**\nNitrogen also has thermodynamic properties that make it useful when welding plastics like ABS and polypropylene; its high heat capacity allows for faster cooling of the welded material without causing warping or cracking due to thermal shock.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd35 6. **Long-Lasting Weld Durability**\nWhen you weld with regular hot air, the oxygen causes oxidation, which weakens the weld and makes it more likely to crack down the road. The nitrogen shielding gas prevents oxidation, ensuring the strongest welds possible \u2014 producing a solid, long-lasting weld that stands up to vibration, cold, and heat.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| Feature | Hot Air Welding | Nitrogen Welding |\n|---|---|---|\n| Oxidation | Yes | No |\n| Smoke/Burns | Significant | Minimal |\n| Weld Strength | Weaker | Stronger |\n| Contamination | Possible | Prevented |\n| Durability | Lower | Higher |\n\nIn short, nitrogen's inert (non-reactive) nature is the key \u2014 it replaces the reactive oxygen at the weld site, allowing clean, strong, and durable plastic fusion.",
